}{}${\lambda ^2}$\end{document} is a positive coefficient. It
implies that the heating-induced temperature anomaly T(x) lags the
heating Q(x) by one-quarter phase, as shown schematically in Fig. 4a. Thus, increased convectional heating can enhance
the upper-tropospheric warm center together with the SAH to the west, intensifying the
sinking motion further to the west. This agrees well with the observed climatic mean
longitudinal distributions of temperature and diabatic heating along the subtropics in the
upper troposphere [63]. During the final two
decades of the 20th century, as the SH over the TP decreased (Fig. 1d) and the precipitation over South China increased (Fig. 4b), the SAH and the upper-tropospheric warm center
intensified (Fig. 4c and d), while precipitation
over North India and Bangladesh decreased (Fig. 4b). Namely, the rainfall anomaly presents a west–east seesaw along the Asian
subtropics (see the two red boxes in Fig. 4b). The
results demonstrate that anomalous heating over the TP exerts an important impact on the
configuration of climate anomalies, at least over the ASM area.
